    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_30c91a8ab862dca2d38f6197ccd0e687e58ed7b23e4a810d4be7b43951f7f2c7",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "A Hecke correspondence can be chosen to set-theoretically separate a proposed finite geodesic singular support: from a point outside the singular set, exactly one branch image lands in it while the remaining branch images avoid it locally. The branchwise Hecke eigenrelation, eigenvalue bound, Cauchy inequality, and shrinking neighborhoods then transport mass control from the absolutely continuous region to the isolated support point, forcing zero limiting mass there and contradicting its membership in the support. This gains a local anti-concentration consequence from nonlocal arithmetic symmetry, but it does not prove full unique equidistribution.",
